---
/*
 * Download page + manual for the Quodlibet Chrome extension.
 *
 * The extension's source lives in its own repo, not here. The served zip is a
 * committed build artifact — to publish a new version:
 *
 *   <quodlibet repo>/pack.sh <this repo>/public/downloads
 *
 * Use the script, not a hand-rolled zip. It builds with `git archive` (tracked
 * files only, which is what keeps the extension's signing key out of a public
 * download), refuses to run against the wrong repo, and verifies the result. Then
 * bump VERSION below and delete the previous zip.
 */
import Admin from '../../layouts/Admin.astro';
import BrutalCard from '../../components/BrutalCard.astro';

const VERSION = '0.0.10';
const ZIP = `/downloads/quodlibet-${VERSION}.zip`;
---

<Admin title="Quodlibet" current="quodlibet">
  <h1>Quodlibet</h1>

  <p class="ym-lede">
    A Chrome extension that turns any number of browser tabs into mixer
    channels. Every tab gets a level, pan, mute and solo — <strong
      >and, since 0.0.9, continuous playback speed, pitch control, seeking and
      transport on any site</strong
    >, not just YouTube. Everything sums into a tape machine you can loop,
    reverse, scrub and stutter in real time, driven by mouse, MIDI controller or
    keyboard. A video mode turns the whole thing into a performance layout that
    fits a single stream frame. <strong
      >New in 0.0.10: record the finished mix to a file, and write a mix down so
      you can find it again.</strong
    >
  </p>

  <BrutalCard
    title={`Download — v${VERSION}`}
    class="ym-download"
    collapsible
    open
  >
    <a class="btn-primary ym-download__btn" href={ZIP} download>
      quodlibet-{VERSION}.zip
    </a>
    <p class="ym-download__note">
      Chrome or Edge only. It is not on the Web Store, so it installs unpacked —
      four steps, below.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Install" collapsible open>
    <ol class="ym-steps">
      <li>
        Download the zip above and unzip it somewhere you will not delete.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open <code>chrome://extensions</code> and turn on <strong
          >Developer mode</strong
        > (top right).
      </li>
      <li>
        Click <strong>Load unpacked</strong> and choose the unzipped
        <code>quodlibet</code> folder.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pin the extension to your toolbar.</strong> Click the puzzle-piece
        icon in Chrome's toolbar and pin Quodlibet. Nothing works without that button
        — see below.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="ym-note">
      Chrome will warn that developer-mode extensions can be a risk, and may
      re-warn on each restart. That is expected for anything not installed from
      the Web Store.
    </p>
    <p class="ym-note">
      <strong>Upgrading?</strong>
      <strong
        >Remove the old entry in
        <code>chrome://extensions</code> first.</strong
      > Every build shares one extension ID, so Chrome refuses to load a second folder
      claiming it while the first is still listed. That includes the oldest builds,
      which were called <em>YouTube Mixer</em> and unzipped to a <code
        >youtube-mixer</code
      >
      folder. Your MIDI mappings and any keys you have rebound survive — Chrome stores
      those, not the folder.
    </p>
    <p class="ym-note">
      <strong>Then reload the tabs you were playing</strong>, not just the
      extension. Chrome does not put the extension's page-side script into tabs
      that were already open, so an already-open tab keeps running the version
      it was given until you reload it — and in 0.0.9 that is the part that
      reaches SoundCloud, Netflix and the rest. Reload the tab, then click the
      toolbar button on it again.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Adding a tab — the one thing you have to know" collapsible>
    <p class="ym-lead-step">
      Play something in a tab, then <strong
        >click the Quodlibet button in your toolbar while that tab is in front</strong
      >. That tab becomes a channel and the mixer opens.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>This is the only way to add a tab.</strong> Chrome will only let an
      extension capture audio from a tab you have explicitly invoked it on, so there
      is no button inside the mixer that can do it for you. One click per tab, once
      per browser session.
    </p>
    <p class="ym-note">
      If you skip this step the mixer stays empty. It will tell you so rather
      than showing you controls that cannot work — every channel listed in the
      mixer is a live capture, so if you can see it, its controls work.
    </p>
    <p>
      A captured tab's own sound is muted and routed through the mixer instead,
      so you hear the mix rather than both. <strong
        >Any audible tab works</strong
      >
      — YouTube, SoundCloud, Bandcamp, Netflix, Crunchyroll, a livestream. Clicking
      the button again on another tab adds it alongside; the mixer is never opened
      twice.
    </p>
    <p class="ym-note">
      <strong>Browsing that tab somewhere else costs you its controls.</strong>
      Chrome grants access per <em>page</em>, not per tab, so navigating to a
      different site takes the extension's reach with it — the audio keeps
      flowing and level, pan, mute and solo carry on, but speed, scrub and
      transport go grey and say so. Click the toolbar button again to get them
      back. Picking the next episode on Netflix does this; so does letting it
      auto-play one.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Finding your way around" collapsible>
    <dl class="ym-defs">
      <dt>help switch</dt>
      <dd>
        Top right of the mixer. Flip it on and hovering anything — any slider,
        button or heading — explains what it does. Flip it off when you no
        longer need it. It remembers which way you left it.
      </dd>

      <dt>video switch</dt>
      <dd>
        Next to help. Swaps between the control layout and the performance
        layout. It remembers which one you left it in.
      </dd>

      <dt>which version you are running</dt>
      <dd>
        Next to the name in the top left. It is read from the extension itself
        rather than written into the page, so it is always the build actually
        loaded — worth checking if something here does not match what you see,
        since Chrome keeps running an unpacked extension from the folder you
        loaded it from.
      </dd>

      <dt>getting the mixer back</dt>
      <dd>
        Click the toolbar button on any tab. If the mixer is already open it
        comes to the front rather than opening a second copy.
      </dd>

      <dt>× on a channel</dt>
      <dd>
        Removes that tab from the mixer and stops capturing it. Pausing a video
        does <em>not</em> remove it — a paused channel stays put and reads
        <em>paused</em> until you play it again.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Channels" collapsible>
    <dl class="ym-defs">
      <dt>level</dt>
      <dd>
        Channel fader, 0 to 1.5 so you can push past unity. Works on any
        captured tab.
      </dd>

      <dt>pan</dt>
      <dd>
        Hard left through centre to hard right. <em>centre</em> resets it.
      </dd>

      <dt>mute / solo</dt>
      <dd>
        Solo is exclusive — clicking one clears the others. <strong
          >Shift-click</strong
        > to solo several at once. Solo ducks everything else without touching their
        faders, so releasing it puts your levels back exactly as they were.
      </dd>

      <dt>rate</dt>
      <dd>
        Continuous playback speed, 0.25× to 2.5×, at whatever precision you drag
        to — <em>1.037 is a real setting</em>, not rounded to a preset.
        <em>1.0</em> snaps back. Works on <strong
          >any site with a player in it</strong
        > since 0.0.9.
      </dd>

      <dt>keep pitch</dt>
      <dd>
        On, speed changes without pitch (time-stretch). Off, pitch rides with
        speed — proper varispeed, the way tape behaves.
      </dd>

      <dt>pos / play / pause</dt>
      <dd>
        Scrub and transport for whatever the tab is playing.
        <strong>Netflix and Crunchyroll cannot be scrubbed</strong> — see below.
      </dd>

      <dt>probe</dt>
      <dd>
        Bottom right of a channel. Copies a short report of what that tab
        actually contains — which player it has and where, and what the
        extension can and cannot reach. <strong
          >If a site does not work, press this and send the result.</strong
        > It is the difference between a fix and a guess: every site fixed in 0.0.9
        was diagnosed from one of these.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <p>
      Rate, position, pitch and transport come from a small script the extension
      puts inside the page when you click the toolbar button. Level, pan, mute
      and solo never touch the page at all — they run on the captured audio, so
      they work on anything, always.
    </p>

    <h3 class="ym-subhead">When those rows go grey</h3>
    <dl class="ym-defs">
      <dt><em>no page control — click the toolbar button on this tab</em></dt>
      <dd>
        The script is not in that tab. Almost always because the tab
        <strong>navigated to a different site</strong> since you added it — Chrome
        grants access per page, not per tab, so browsing away takes it with you. Click
        the toolbar button again and the row comes back. The mixer tries to repair
        this by itself first and only says so when Chrome refuses.
      </dd>

      <dt><em>no scrub — protected</em></dt>
      <dd>
        A DRM stream: <strong>Netflix, Crunchyroll</strong> and anything else that
        decrypts as it plays. Those players manage their own buffer, and a seek written
        underneath one is indistinguishable from corruption — Netflix answers with
        its <em>Pardon the interruption</em> error page and the only way out is reloading
        the tab. So the scrubber refuses rather than breaking your stream. <strong
          >Speed, pitch and transport still work</strong
        >; they are things the player does, not places it is asked to be.
      </dd>

      <dt><em>nothing here to drive</em></dt>
      <dd>
        The script is in the page and found no player it can reach — a site
        built on raw Web Audio, or one whose player sits inside an embedded
        frame. Level, pan, mute and solo still work. Press <strong>probe</strong
        >
        and send it; the report says which of those it is.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Tape" collapsible>
    <p>
      The tape is always recording the mix — a rolling 60 seconds. Nothing you
      do to it affects what the tabs are playing, so the sources keep running
      underneath whatever you are doing.
    </p>
    <dl class="ym-defs">
      <dt>thru / tape</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>thru</strong> passes audio straight out with no delay while still
        filling the buffer. <strong>tape</strong> plays from the tape head, which
        sits a quarter second behind live so there is something to scrub back into.
      </dd>

      <dt>speed</dt>
      <dd>
        −2× to +2×, continuous. Negative is reverse. Zero is a dead stop.
        Sustained speeds above 1 will catch up to live and stick there — you
        cannot play faster than the tab is recording.
      </dd>

      <dt>reverse / stop / 1.0</dt>
      <dd>
        Shortcuts for −1, 0 and normal speed. <strong
          >These set the speed, not the position.</strong
        >
        <em>1.0</em> means "play forwards at normal speed from wherever the head currently
        is" — which after a reverse is somewhere in the past.
        <br />
        Whichever one matches the current speed <strong>lights up</strong>, so
        you can always see which way the transport is running. Move the speed
        slider to something in between and none of them light — the slider has
        it.
      </dd>

      <dt>reverse, again</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>reverse</strong> latches, so clicking it a second time releases it
        and returns you to normal forward speed. It deliberately leaves the head where
        it is: reverse changed the speed, so releasing it changes the speed back and
        nothing else. You are still in the past — <em>to live</em> is what brings
        you back, and it stays amber until you press it.
      </dd>

      <dt>to live</dt>
      <dd>
        Jumps the head back to the present. This is how you undo a reverse — see
        below, it is the single most confusing thing about the tape.
      </dd>

      <dt>rew / ff</dt>
      <dd>
        Hold to shuttle at 4×. Release and it resumes normal speed
        <em>where it landed</em>, like a real transport.
      </dd>

      <dt>stutter</dt>
      <dd>
        Hold to loop the last 60, 125, 250 or 500 ms. Release and it snaps back
        to live, so a stutter is something you come back from. It also restores
        any loop you already had set.
      </dd>

      <dt>loop</dt>
      <dd>
        Latching. Grabs the last 1, 2, 4 or 8 seconds and repeats it until you
        press <em>off</em>.
      </dd>

      <dt>cubic</dt>
      <dd>
        Interpolation quality for off-speed playback. Linear by default; cubic
        is smoother and costs a little more. The difference is subtle and most
        obvious at odd speeds.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="ym-note">
      The status line under the controls reports the mode, how far behind live
      the tape head is, and how much of the 60-second buffer is filled.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard
    title="Behind live — read this before you use reverse"
    collapsible
  >
    <p class="ym-lead-step">
      Reverse leaves you in the past, and normal speed never brings you back.
      <strong>to live</strong> does.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>reverse</strong> latches. While it is running the gap between the tape
      head and the present opens at <strong
        >two seconds for every second you hold it</strong
      > — the head walks backwards while the tabs keep recording forwards. Ten seconds
      of reverse puts you twenty seconds in the past, up to the 60-second limit of
      the buffer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since v0.0.8 the button lights up while it is running, and clicking it
      again releases it — but that only returns the <em>speed</em> to normal. The
      head stays where it walked to.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pressing <em>1.0</em> afterwards does not undo that either. At normal speed
      the tape head advances at exactly the speed the recording does, so the gap is
      held open indefinitely. Only four things close it: <strong>to live</strong
      >
      and <strong>thru</strong> (instantly), releasing a <strong>stutter</strong
      >, or a speed above 1 — and that last one only claws back
      <em>speed minus one</em> seconds per second, so 1.5× needs eight seconds to
      recover a four-second gap.
    </p>
    <p class="ym-note">
      <strong>Why this matters more than it sounds.</strong> Nothing about the sound
      tells you it is happening. You hear the mix as it was, and every change you
      make from then on — a fader, a speed, a mute — is recorded immediately but does
      not become audible until seconds later. In video mode it is worse: the picture
      stays live while the audio sits in the past, so they drift apart and stay there.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the mixer says so. Once you are more than half a second behind,
      <strong>to live</strong> turns amber and pulses, and the status line turns amber
      with it. Both go back to normal the moment you are level with the present.
    </p>
    <p class="ym-note">
      This is deliberate rather than a limitation. Playing forwards at normal
      speed from ten seconds ago is a real move — it is how you play back
      something you just caught — so the tape does not silently yank you to the
      present just because you asked for normal speed.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Video mode" collapsible>
    <p>
      The <strong>video</strong> switch at the top right swaps the mixer for a performance
      layout: every channel becomes a video tile showing that tab's picture, with
      its controls laid over the bottom edge. Same channels, same tape, same MIDI
      bindings underneath — only the presentation changes, and switching back and
      forth disturbs nothing.
    </p>
    <p class="ym-note">
      A 2×2 grid on a 16:9 screen gives cells that are 2:1 — wider than the 16:9
      picture inside them — so every tile has spare width down its sides that
      the video could never use. That is where the controls go: a tall fader
      either side rather than a thin strip along the bottom, because side pixels
      are nearly free while bottom pixels cost the video both height and width.
    </p>
    <dl class="ym-defs">
      <dt>the tiles</dt>
      <dd>
        Cropped to the player, so you get the video and not the sidebar,
        comments and page furniture. Because it is the real tab rather than an
        embed, the real end screen and its suggested videos show up too.
      </dd>

      <dt>level and speed</dt>
      <dd>
        The two faders down the left of each tile, running its full height for
        maximum throw. Speed is vertical for the same reason a turntable's pitch
        fader is: it puts both things you ride under one hand.
      </dd>

      <dt>meter, buttons, pan</dt>
      <dd>
        Down the right: the channel's live level, then mute, solo, keep-pitch
        and remove, with pan across the foot of the column.
      </dd>

      <dt>title bar</dt>
      <dd>
        Along the bottom of each tile, with the video's progress drawn behind
        the title. <strong>Drag it to seek.</strong> The title is outlined so it stays
        readable over the fill.
      </dd>

      <dt>audio-only channels</dt>
      <dd>
        A tab with no video — Bandcamp, a podcast — gets a waveform drawn from
        its own audio rather than a blank tile.
      </dd>

      <dt>fullscreen</dt>
      <dd>
        Fills the screen, so a capture picks up the grid and no browser chrome.
      </dd>

      <dt>clean</dt>
      <dd>
        Hides the overlays for a moment and shows pure footage. The controls
        keep working, they are just not drawn.
      </dd>

      <dt>HQ</dt>
      <dd>
        Video quality. See below — leave it off unless you need the detail.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="ym-note">
      The controls deliberately do <em>not</em> fade out after a few seconds the way
      video players usually do. On a performance stream the faders moving are half
      the point of watching.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Video quality" collapsible>
    <p>
      Four videos at once is real work for a laptop, and it is being paid twice:
      each tab is decoding YouTube at whatever quality it chose, and the mixer
      is then capturing that tab's picture on top. <strong>HQ</strong>, next to
      <em>clean</em>, moves both halves together.
    </p>
    <dl class="ym-defs">
      <dt>off (default)</dt>
      <dd>
        Capture runs at 426×240, 15fps, and each YouTube tab is asked to play at
        its lowest quality. Fine for monitoring and for anything the audience is
        going to see re-encoded anyway.
      </dd>

      <dt>on</dt>
      <dd>
        Capture at 960×540, 30fps, and the tabs go back to choosing their own
        quality. Use it when you are actually streaming or want to look closely
        at something.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="ym-note">
      Switching does not interrupt anything — no channel drops out and no audio
      gaps. Setting the tabs' own quality reaches into YouTube's player, so on a
      page that has changed shape underneath us it may quietly do nothing; the
      capture side always applies.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Saving a mix" collapsible>
    <p class="ym-lead-step">
      A mix is four tabs, each at some moment, blended at some levels, through a
      tape at some speed. Close the window and there is no way back to it — you
      cannot bookmark <em>these four, at those moments</em>. That is what
      <strong>snapshot</strong> is for.
    </p>

    <p>
      Press <strong>snapshot</strong> in the Mix section (or <code>y</code>, or
      the button beside the tape in video mode). Chrome asks where to put the
      file. Inside it you get <strong>both halves at once</strong>: the top is a
      readable list you can open in any text editor, or glance at with Quick
      Look; below a marker line sits the data <strong>restore</strong> reads back.
    </p>

    <pre
      class="ym-sample">quodlibet mix — Aug 19, 2026 4:12 PM

1. Lavender Country — Cryin&#39; These Cocksucking Tears
   https://lavendercountry.bandcamp.com/track/…
   at 2:41 of 4:02 · lvl 0.82 · L20

2. Blade Runner Blues
   https://youtube.com/watch?v=…
   at 30:41 · lvl 0.55 · centre · MUTED

tape: thru · 1.00x forward · cubic
quodlibet 0.0.10

── quodlibet data, do not edit below ──
&#123;"app":"quodlibet","snapshot":1,…&#125;</pre>

    <p>
      <strong>Notes you type above the list survive a reload</strong>, so a
      snapshot is a reasonable place to write down what you were going for.
    </p>

    <h3 class="ym-subhead">Restoring one</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>restore…</strong> opens a file picker. Quodlibet reopens those tabs
      and, as each one is captured, puts its level, pan, mute, solo, speed and position
      back on.
    </p>
    <p class="ym-note">
      <strong>It cannot capture them for you.</strong> Chrome only allows a capture
      from a real click on the toolbar button, one tab at a time, so a restore is
      half-automatic by design: Quodlibet opens the tabs, you click the button on
      each, and the channel snaps into place as it arrives. A
      <strong>waiting list</strong> names the ones still owed, and survives closing
      the mixer — four tabs is four trips to the toolbar.
    </p>
    <p>
      A YouTube link gets its timestamp written in, so the tab lands near the
      right moment before it is even captured. No other site is guessed at:
      every one spells that differently and a wrong guess is worse than none.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Recording" collapsible>
    <p class="ym-lead-step">
      <strong>● record</strong> in the Mix section, <code>R</code> on the keyboard,
      or the button beside the tape in video mode. Press it again to stop, and the
      take lands in your downloads.
    </p>

    <p>
      It records <strong>after the tape</strong> — so a reverse pass, a stutter, a
      speed ride are all baked into the file. The recording is the performance, not
      the ingredients.
    </p>

    <p>
      Starting and stopping is <strong>inaudible</strong>. The recorder is a
      second tap hung off the output, never in the path of it, so you can hit
      record in the middle of something good without a click or a gap.
    </p>

    <p class="ym-note">
      <strong>The button pulses red while it runs</strong>, in both layouts, and
      the line beneath it counts the minutes and the megabytes. That is
      deliberate: a take you have forgotten you are making is the way this
      feature goes wrong.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>WebM/Opus, with no length limit.</strong> Those two facts are the same
      fact — Chrome encodes as it goes, so an hour costs no more memory than a minute.
      It plays in any browser and in VLC; <strong
        >Logic wants it converted first</strong
      >. If that conversion becomes the annoying part, say so and a WAV option
      can be added — it would buy the drag-and-drop in exchange for a hard
      twenty-minute ceiling.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Casting to a TV" collapsible>
    <p class="ym-lead-step">
      The mix comes out of <strong>the mixer tab itself</strong>. So casting
      that one tab sends the sound and the video grid together, while the
      captured tabs play on in the background, unheard directly.
    </p>

    <ol class="ym-steps">
      <li>Press <strong>set up for casting</strong> in the Cast section.</li>
      <li>
        Open Chrome&#39;s <strong>⋮</strong> menu →
        <strong>Cast, save and share</strong> → <strong>Cast</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Pick your device, then set <strong>Sources</strong> to
        <strong>Cast tab</strong>.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      <strong>set up for casting</strong> does the part that is Quodlibet&#39;s: switches
      to the video grid, turns high quality on, hides the overlays and goes fullscreen
      — the state actually worth mirroring.
    </p>

    <p class="ym-note">
      <strong>Chrome does the casting, not the extension</strong>, and that is
      not a shortcut taken. An extension is not allowed to start a cast: there
      is no API for it, and the two web APIs that come close both refuse a live
      audio stream. So the last three steps are yours and there is no button
      that can do them.
      <strong>None of this has been tried against real hardware.</strong> If the menu
      does not behave as described, that is worth reporting rather than working around.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="MIDI" collapsible>
    <p>
      Faders, knobs and pads can drive the mixer. Press <strong
        >enable MIDI</strong
      >
      once — Chrome asks permission for all MIDI access, and remembers your answer.
    </p>
    <ol class="ym-steps">
      <li>Turn on <strong>learn</strong>.</li>
      <li>
        Click the control you want to bind — a level, a pan, a rate, mute or
        solo. While learn is on, clicking arms a control instead of moving it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Move the fader or press the pad you want to drive it with. That's the
        binding made.
      </li>
      <li>Turn <strong>learn</strong> back off and play.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="ym-note">
      Bindings are saved against <strong>channel position</strong>, not against
      the tab. Fader 1 drives channel 1 today and tomorrow, and if you remove
      the top channel the one below it moves up into that fader. Binding against
      a tab would not survive a restart, because Chrome renumbers tabs every
      session.
    </p>
    <p>
      Knobs and faders map across their full range. Pads and buttons fire once
      on press, so holding one doesn't retrigger. Your mappings are listed under
      the MIDI section with an <em>×</em> to forget any of them, and they survive
      closing the browser — they are shown even before you enable MIDI, so a restart
      doesn't look like they were lost.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Keyboard" collapsible>
    <p class="ym-lead-step">
      Press <strong><code>?</code></strong> in the mixer. That list is the manual
      for this section — it is generated from the bindings themselves, so it cannot
      fall out of date the way a printed one does.
    </p>

    <p class="ym-note">
      <strong>Nobody has performed with this yet.</strong> It passes its tests — 64
      of them — but no one has had it under their hands during a set, and the defaults
      for the Quodlibet-specific keys are considered guesses rather than decisions.
      Move anything that sits wrong (see <em>rebinding</em> below) and say what you
      changed; there is an open question about the grammar on
      <strong>issue #3</strong> in the extension repo.
    </p>

    <p>
      The grammar is <a href="https://github.com/doo-nothing/los">los</a>'s,
      because Tube asked for it by name and because it is a real grammar rather
      than a list of shortcuts: <strong
        >navigate along the layout, adjust across it</strong
      >; a number typed first repeats what follows (<code>3j</code> moves three);
      the shifted form of an adjust key is a bigger step.
    </p>

    <h3 class="ym-subhead">The one idea to hold on to</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>The tape is the last strip.</strong>
      <code>h</code> and
      <code>l</code> walk left and right through your channels and then onto the tape,
      exactly the way los's own mixer walks onto MASTER. So the tape's speed, loop
      and interpolation sit under the same keys as a channel's level and pan, and
      there is no separate mode to be in without realising.
    </p>

    <dl class="ym-defs">
      <dt>h · l</dt>
      <dd>
        Previous / next strip — the channels, then the tape, wrapping around.
      </dd>

      <dt>j · k</dt>
      <dd>
        Next / previous <em>param</em> within the selected strip. A channel has level,
        pan, speed, position; the tape has speed, loop, interpolation.
      </dd>

      <dt>K · J <span class="ym-alt">(or = and -)</span></dt>
      <dd>Raise / lower it by 1%. <code>3K</code> does it three times.</dd>

      <dt>+ · _</dt>
      <dd>The coarse version, 5%.</dd>

      <dt>0</dt>
      <dd>Reset that param — level to 1.00, pan to centre, speed to 1.000.</dd>

      <dt>gg · G · gt2</dt>
      <dd>First channel · the tape · go to channel 2.</dd>

      <dt>space</dt>
      <dd>
        Play or pause the selected channel. On the tape strip it switches
        <em>thru</em> and <em>tape</em> instead.
      </dd>

      <dt>m · s · v</dt>
      <dd>
        Mute · solo · pitch mode. On the tape strip, <code>v</code> is reverse and
        <code>s</code> stops the head.
      </dd>

      <dt>[ · ]</dt>
      <dd>
        Seek back / forward five seconds. <strong>Held</strong> on the tape strip
        they shuttle at 4× and let go where they land, like a real transport.
      </dd>

      <dt>q · w · e · r</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Held</strong> on the tape strip: stutter the last 60, 125, 250 or
        500 milliseconds. Release and it snaps back.
      </dd>

      <dt>dd</dt>
      <dd>
        Remove the selected channel from the mixer. Two presses, deliberately.
      </dd>

      <dt>y</dt>
      <dd>
        Snapshot the mix — vi&#39;s <em>yank</em>, and a snapshot is a yank.
        Works from any strip.
      </dd>

      <dt>R</dt>
      <dd>Start or stop recording. Also from any strip.</dd>
    </dl>

    <p class="ym-note">
      A strip below the tape says what is selected — <em
        >keys channel 1 · level 1.000</em
      > — and the selected channel is outlined in both layouts, so the keyboard is
      never acting somewhere you cannot see.
      <strong>Typing into a control does not move anything</strong>: click a
      fader and the keys leave it alone until you click away.
    </p>

    <h3 class="ym-subhead">Rebinding</h3>
    <p>
      Every key can be moved, the same way MIDI already works. Press
      <code>?</code>, turn on <strong>learn</strong>, click the line you want,
      then press the key. <strong>reset all</strong> puts everything back. Your changes
      are saved beside your MIDI mappings and survive a restart, and the list shows
      moved keys in accent so an edited keyboard reads at a glance.
    </p>
    <p>
      Taking a key that is already in use <strong
        >unbinds whatever had it</strong
      >
      rather than leaving two actions fighting over one press — the displaced line
      reads <em>unbound</em> until you give it something. A few keys refuse to move
      and say why: digits belong to counts, and <code>gg</code>,
      <code>gt#</code>, <code>dd</code>, <code>Esc</code> and <code>?</code>
      itself stay put, since rebinding the way back into the list is a way to lose
      it.
    </p>
  </BrutalCard>

  <BrutalCard title="Known limits" collapsible>
    <ul class="ym-limits">
      <li>
        <strong>Chrome and Edge only.</strong> Safari and Firefox cannot capture tab
        audio at all.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One toolbar click per tab</strong>, every session. Chrome's
        rule, not ours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Protected streams cannot be scrubbed.</strong> Netflix, Crunchyroll
        and anything else using DRM manage their own buffer, and a seek written underneath
        one takes the page down — Netflix throws its
        <em>Pardon the interruption</em> page and wants a reload. The scrubber refuses
        instead and says <em>no scrub — protected</em>. Speed, pitch and
        transport are unaffected.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A player inside an embedded frame is out of reach.</strong> A Bandcamp
        or YouTube player dropped into someone else's page sits in a frame the extension
        cannot enter, so that channel captures audio but offers no transport. Bandcamp,
        SoundCloud, Netflix, Crunchyroll and Bandcamp Daily on their own sites are
        all fine — it is embeds specifically. Press <strong>probe</strong> on one
        and send it if this matters to you; it is measured, not fixed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Browsing a captured tab to a different site drops its controls</strong
        > until you click the toolbar button again. Chrome grants access per page.
        The mixer repairs this by itself where Chrome permits and tells you when it
        cannot.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A YouTube ad hijacks the deck.</strong> Pre-rolls swap in their own
        player, so rate and seek would act on the advert. The deck labels this
        <em>ad playing</em> — wait it out.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Netflix channels are called "Netflix".</strong> Its watch pages carry
        no episode name anywhere the extension can read, so the tile cannot name what
        is playing. Crunchyroll and the rest name themselves properly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The tape adds a quarter second of delay</strong> once engaged. Inaudible
        by itself, visible against picture.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A stopped tape is silent.</strong> Pressing <em>stop</em> mutes the
        tape output rather than holding the last moment of sound — the same as a real
        transport. The sources keep playing underneath, which is what lets break,
        loop and stutter rejoin them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Closing or reloading the mixer ends the mix.</strong> The audio lives
        in the mixer tab, so shutting it stops the captures and every tab goes back
        to playing normally by itself. Changed in 0.0.4 — it used to keep running
        invisibly in the background.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The keyboard is untried in performance.</strong> It passes its tests;
        nobody has played a set with it. Treat the Quodlibet-specific keys as provisional
        and move what does not fit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>MIDI needs permission every fresh profile.</strong> Chrome has asked
        for all MIDI access since version 124, not just for devices that can be reflashed.
        One prompt, once.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Closing a captured tab drops its channel.</strong> Re-add it with
        the toolbar button. Pausing does not — a paused channel stays in the mixer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Restoring a snapshot cannot capture the tabs for you.</strong> It
        reopens them and puts every setting back as each one arrives, but the toolbar
        click is Chrome&#39;s rule and there is no way around it. The waiting list
        tells you which are still owed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recordings are WebM, so Logic wants a conversion.</strong> That is
        the price of having no length limit — see <em>Recording</em>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Casting is Chrome&#39;s, and untested here.</strong> An extension
        cannot start a cast, so the Cast section prepares the panel and hands you
        to the browser&#39;s menu. Nobody has yet pointed it at a real device.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </BrutalCard>
</Admin>

